The following classes are already defined:

class Enemy:
    def __init__(self, health):
        self.health = health

class Goblin(Enemy):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__(30)

class Dragon(Enemy):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__(200)

class Bat(Enemy):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__(10)

Using the factory design pattern, construct a Python function create_enemies(names) that takes a list of type-name strings (for example ["bat", "Goblin", "DRAGON"]) and returns a list of new enemy objects of the matching classes, in the same order. Matching must ignore upper/lower case. If any name is not one of the three types, raise a ValueError and return no list.

solution:
def create_enemies(names):
    makers = {"goblin": Goblin, "dragon": Dragon, "bat": Bat}
    enemies = []
    for name in names:
        key = name.lower()
        if key not in makers:
            raise ValueError("Unknown enemy type: " + name)
        enemies.append(makers[key]())
    return enemies

